Answer unexpected errors instead of dropping the connection

keystone/server/flask/application.py sets PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS=True and
registers error handlers only for the exceptions of
keystone.exception.KEYSTONE_API_EXCEPTIONS and for TypeError. Any other
exception a view raises leaves the Flask application, passes every
middleware and reaches the WSGI server, and uWSGI writes no response
for it. On a keep-alive connection of uWSGI's HTTP router
(--http-keepalive, as the keystone-operator renders it) the client then
waits for the router's 60 second socket timeout and sees the connection
closed. The token issue path hits this while the database is
unreachable: POST /v3/auth/tokens reads it through keystone/auth/core.py
and the resource SQL backend, oslo.db raises DBConnectionError,
Flask-RESTful 0.3.10's error_router re-raises it into Flask's
handle_user_exception, and Flask finds no handler. In the
e2e-chaos-random run 41903435759 of CobaltCore, Keystone logged
"Unhandled error: oslo_db.exception.DBConnectionError" and
"POST /v3/auth/tokens => generated 0 bytes in 79 msecs (HTTP/1.1 500)"
after openstack-db-0 was killed, and a starting nova-compute got
"Remote end closed connection without response" 60 seconds later.
Token validation does not escape: middleware_exceptions in
keystone/server/flask/request_processing/middleware/auth_context.py
wraps AuthContextMiddleware.process_request and answers a JSON 500.

The application now registers _handle_unknown_keystone_exception for
Exception as well. Flask picks the most specific registered class, so
keystone's own exceptions keep _handle_keystone_exception and TypeError
keeps its 400. The handler returns werkzeug's HTTP errors (an unrouted
path's 404) unchanged, answers DBConnectionError with
exception.ServiceUnavailable (503) and every other exception with
exception.UnexpectedError, a JSON 500 whose message names the
exception only when insecure_debug is set. middleware_exceptions
answers DBConnectionError with ServiceUnavailable as well, so a lost
database answers 503 on the issue and the validation path alike;
keystoneauth retries a 503 when a caller sets status_code_retries.
Every other oslo.db error (DBDeadlock, DBError) keeps the 500.
_handle_keystone_exception logs the 503 and the 500 with LOG.exception
inside the except block Flask calls it from, so the DBConnectionError
traceback stays in the log.

PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS stays True. Setting it to False would make
Flask-RESTful 0.3.10 answer every non-HTTP exception on keystone's
routes with {"message": "Internal Server Error"} before a registered
handler runs, keystone's own API errors (400, 401, 404, 409) included.
With a handler for Exception the setting only governs an exception
raised inside a handler.

Keystone 30.0.0 already defines exception.ServiceUnavailable (upstream
commit 0e2166742f, "feat: Register ec2credential as a marker auth
method", which raises it when the ec2credential auth method is not
enabled). The patch raises that class and defines none.

No upstream test asserts that an exception escapes the application or
that middleware_exceptions answers 500 for a database error, so no
existing test moves. test_unrouted_path_is_not_jsonified_404 removes
the test application's 404 handler, so its unrouted 404 now reaches the
Exception handler; the test stays unchanged and pins the pass-through.
The patch adds TestKeystoneFlaskUnexpectedErrors to
keystone/tests/unit/server/test_keystone_flask.py, whose four tests
make authenticate_for_token raise: DBConnectionError answers 503,
DBDeadlock and RuntimeError answer a JSON 500 without the exception's
text, and TypeError still answers 400. It adds two tests to
AuthContextMiddlewareTest in keystone/tests/unit/test_middleware.py
whose fetch_token raises: DBConnectionError answers 503, RuntimeError
500. Without the code hunks test_lost_database_answers_503,
test_other_database_error_answers_500,
test_unexpected_error_answers_json_500 and
test_lost_database_during_validation_answers_503 fail.

test_lost_database_patch_applied in
tests/container-images/verify_keystone.sh runs the built image under
the keystone-operator's uWSGI command against a database address that
refuses connections and asserts that POST /v3/auth/tokens answers 503
within ten seconds.
The 2026.1 twin,
patches/keystone/2026.1/0003-answer-unexpected-errors-instead-of-dropping-the-connection.patch,
carries the same hunks and backports the class.

Applies to keystone 30.0.0 (2026.2).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

diff --git a/keystone/server/flask/application.py b/keystone/server/flask/application.py
index ba8ca8360..cffa33a26 100644
--- a/keystone/server/flask/application.py
+++ b/keystone/server/flask/application.py
@@ -14,9 +14,11 @@ import functools
 import sys
 
 import flask
+from oslo_db import exception as db_exception
 import oslo_i18n
 from oslo_log import log
 from oslo_middleware import healthcheck
+from werkzeug import exceptions as werkzeug_exceptions
 from werkzeug.middleware import dispatcher
 
 from keystone.api import auth
@@ -174,10 +176,19 @@ def _handle_keystone_exception(error):
 
 
 def _handle_unknown_keystone_exception(error):
+    # The handler is registered for Exception, so werkzeug's own HTTP errors
+    # (an unrouted path's 404) reach it too; they keep the response werkzeug
+    # renders.
+    if isinstance(error, werkzeug_exceptions.HTTPException):
+        return error
     # translate a python exception to something we can properly render as
     # an API error.
     if isinstance(error, TypeError):
         new_exc = exception.ValidationError(error)
+    elif isinstance(error, db_exception.DBConnectionError):
+        # The database is unreachable: answer 503, which keystoneauth
+        # retries when a caller sets status_code_retries, instead of a 500.
+        new_exc = exception.ServiceUnavailable()
     else:
         new_exc = exception.UnexpectedError(error)
     return _handle_keystone_exception(new_exc)
@@ -206,6 +217,11 @@ def application_factory(name='public'):
     # specifically TypeError. It will render as a 400 error, but presented in
     # a "web-ified" manner
     app.register_error_handler(TypeError, _handle_unknown_keystone_exception)
+    # Any other exception renders as a 500 error, or a 503 error when the
+    # database is unreachable; werkzeug's own HTTP errors (404, 405) keep
+    # their answer. Without a handler an exception escapes to the WSGI
+    # server, which sends no response at all.
+    app.register_error_handler(Exception, _handle_unknown_keystone_exception)
 
     # Add core before request functions
     app.before_request(req_logging.log_request_info)
